---
import {
	BATCH_BOARDS,
	COST_LIMIT_USD,
	MODELS as MODEL_IDS,
	REASONING_EFFORT,
	WALL_CLOCK_LIMIT_HOURS
} from '@pcbbench/bench/runs';
import { GATE_PENALTY, GROUPS, ITEMS, groupPoints } from '@pcbbench/bench/scoring';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { MODELS } from '../data/catalog';
import { GITHUB, runs } from '../data/runs';
import { GROUP_LABELS, ITEM_LABELS } from '../data/scoring';

const judges = [...new Set(runs.map((run) => MODELS[run.review.judge].name))];
const usualLimit = Math.min(...Object.values(COST_LIMIT_USD));
const higherLimits = MODEL_IDS.filter((model) => COST_LIMIT_USD[model] !== usualLimit);
---

<Base
	title="Method · PCBBench"
	description="How PCBBench runs AI agents on PCB design tasks and scores the boards they deliver."
>
	<article class="page">
		<header class="intro">
			<h1>Method</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				Each run gives one agent one task prompt. Scripts check the board it delivers, an AI judge
				reviews it, and PCBWay prices it. The score covers the board only.
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="section">
			<h2>A run</h2>
			<ol class="steps">
				<li>
					The agent gets the task prompt with its model and {REASONING_EFFORT} reasoning effort filled
					in, a clean machine with KiCad, and up to {WALL_CLOCK_LIMIT_HOURS} hours. AI spend is capped
					at ${usualLimit}
					{
						higherLimits.map((model) => (
							<>
								{' '}
								(${COST_LIMIT_USD[model]} for {MODELS[model].name})
							</>
						))
					}.
				</li>
				<li>The run's work folder, logs, tokens and AI cost are collected.</li>
				<li>
					Scripts run DRC and ERC with the run's own KiCad version, match the fab files to the board,
					and check the BOM, placement file, parts, gates and layout.
				</li>
				<li>{judges.join(' and ')} reviews an anonymous copy of the board.</li>
				<li>
					PCBWay quotes 1 and {BATCH_BOARDS} assembled boards, after any fixes needed to order them.
				</li>
			</ol>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<h2>Score</h2>
			<p>
				Each item earns its points times a fraction from 0 to 1. A board that fails a gate loses{' '}
				{GATE_PENALTY} points. Differences under about 5 points aren't meaningful. "Change the mix" on
				the leaderboard reweights the groups.
			</p>
			<div class="scroll">
				<table class="items">
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th scope="col">Item</th>
							<th scope="col" class="n">Points</th>
							<th scope="col">Fraction</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					{
						GROUPS.map((group) => (
							<tbody style={`--color: var(--${group})`}>
								<tr class="group">
									<th scope="rowgroup" colspan="3">
										<span class="swatch" aria-hidden="true" />
										{GROUP_LABELS[group]} {groupPoints(group)}
									</th>
								</tr>
								{ITEMS.filter((item) => item.group === group).map((item) => (
									<tr>
										<td>{ITEM_LABELS[item.name].label}</td>
										<td class="n">{item.points}</td>
										<td class="fraction">{ITEM_LABELS[item.name].fraction}</td>
									</tr>
								))}
							</tbody>
						))
					}
				</table>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<h2>Gates</h2>
			<ul class="facts">
				<li>
					<strong>Prompt features.</strong> The BOM has a part for every feature the prompt asks for.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Crystal.</strong> Its load capacitors are within 25% of what its datasheet needs, with
					3 pF of board capacitance.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Buttons.</strong> BOOT pulls QSPI_SS to ground through a switch, and RUN has its own
					switch to ground.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Power path.</strong> The charger takes USB VBUS, charges the battery connector and
					feeds the 3.3 V regulator.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>No fatal finding.</strong> The judge found nothing that stops the assembled board
					working, damages a part or stops PCBWay building it.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<h2>Checks</h2>
			<div class="cards">
				<div style="--color: var(--designReview)">
					<h3>Design review</h3>
					<p>
						{judges.join(' and ')} on high reasoning, in the Sprocket CLI with its KiCad connector, sees
						the KiCad project, the BOM, renders and layer images, without run names. It lists layout and
						routing problems with their parts, nets and evidence, rated minor, major or fatal. It gives
						no scores.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div style="--color: var(--circuitChecks)">
					<h3>Circuit checks</h3>
					<p>
						Scripts read the board file for the nearest capacitor to each RP2350 power pin, ESD parts
						and series resistors on USB D+ and D−, copper in the Wi-Fi antenna keepout, and the current
						battery dividers draw when the board is off.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div style="--color: var(--layoutChecks)">
					<h3>Layout checks</h3>
					<p>
						Vias in the exposed pads of the RP2350, charger and regulator. USB, QSPI and microSD track
						length over a ground plane on the next layer. Silkscreen text height. Board area.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div style="--color: var(--orderable)">
					<h3>Orderable</h3>
					<p>
						Fab options PCBWay doesn't offer, DRC errors or fab files that differ from the board, and
						BOM lines with invalid part numbers, too little stock for {BATCH_BOARDS} boards or a footprint
						that doesn't match the part.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div style="--color: var(--partsData)">
					<h3>Parts data</h3>
					<p>
						Parts with a 3D model that loads, and BOM lines with a valid LCSC, Digi-Key or Mouser part
						number.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="section">
			<h2>Not in the score</h2>
			<ul class="facts">
				<li><strong>AI cost.</strong> The run's API cost at market prices, including subagents.</li>
				<li><strong>Time.</strong> How long the agent was working.</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Board price.</strong> PCB, assembly and parts at PCBWay's instant-quote prices,
					without shipping. PCBWay makes at least 5 PCBs, so 1 board means 5 PCBs with 1 assembled.
					Parts are priced at distributor list prices.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The <a href={`${GITHUB}#readme`}>README</a> has every check's exact rules, and the{' '}
				<a href={`${GITHUB}/tree/main/bench`}>bench code</a> runs them.
			</p>
		</section>
	</article>
</Base>

<style>
	.intro {
		display: grid;
		gap: 1rem;
		padding-top: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 4rem);
	}

	h1 {
		font-size: var(--step-4);
	}

	.steps,
	.facts {
		display: grid;
		gap: 0.6rem;
		margin: 0;
		padding-left: 1.25em;
		max-width: var(--measure);
	}

	.scroll {
		overflow-x: auto;
	}

	.items {
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 56rem;
	}

	.items th,
	.items td {
		text-align: left;
		vertical-align: top;
		padding: 0.45rem 1rem 0.45rem 0;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
	}

	.items thead th {
		color: var(--dim);
		font-weight: normal;
		font-size: var(--step--1);
	}

	.items .n {
		text-align: right;
	}

	.items .group th {
		padding-top: 1.25rem;
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.fraction {
		color: var(--dim);
		font-size: var(--step--1);
	}

	.swatch {
		display: inline-block;
		width: 0.75rem;
		height: 0.75rem;
		margin-right: 0.5rem;
		border-radius: 2px;
		background: var(--color);
	}

	.cards {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 18rem), 1fr));
		gap: 1.5rem 2.5rem;
	}

	.cards > div {
		display: grid;
		align-content: start;
		gap: 0.5rem;
		border-top: 3px solid var(--color);
		padding-top: 0.75rem;
	}

	.cards h3 {
		font-size: var(--step-1);
	}

	.cards p {
		color: var(--dim);
	}
</style>
